    slug: "spider-noir-inceleme",
    title: "'Spider-Noir' Review: Spider-Man Is Finally Dirty and Weird Again",
    excerpt: "Prime Video's Nicolas Cage-led 'Spider-Noir' is a sly, stylized noir take that deliberately breaks from the \"clean\" version of the superhero genre — a show far better than it needed to be.",
    body: "Nicolas Cage's 1930s Spider-Man arrives on Prime Video's 'Spider-Noir' not just as a black-and-white image, but as a fully realized character. The show deliberately reverses the \"clean, universal, family-friendly\" version of the superhero genre we've seen over the past decade: a grimy New York, a whiskey-soaked detective office, and a Peter Parker willing to get genuinely strange.\n\nCage's performance turns the show into an ongoing spectacle. He balances the character's melancholy with a comic swagger, carrying both film noir's cold, observant voice and the grotesque energy of the comics at once. The supporting cast is strong too, particularly a dark cabaret singer who signals the show's full commitment to its genre ambitions.\n\nVisuals are the show's biggest strength. The monochrome palette of rain-soaked streets, sequences where spider-sense is highlighted with yellow-red accents, and its selective use of color — 'Spider-Noir' doesn't feel like a Marvel side project, but like a period noir shot with genuine cinematic seriousness. The action scenes are short and hard-hitting, following the rhythm of a gangster film rather than typical superhero choreography.\n\nIt has its flaws: the pace slows in some episodes, and scenes that lean too heavily on mythology borrow from the show's own tone. But overall, 'Spider-Noir' may not be the boldest superhero adaptation of recent years, but it is the most original. It's a reminder that things can be different in this genre — and that alone matters.",
